778
00:28:35.120 --> 00:28:36.300
Oh, my gosh.

779
00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:37.720
Full circle life moment.

780
00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:39.800
Guys, my bread is very good.

781
00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:40.380
Thank you.

782
00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:52.020
Also, we cannot not talk about the fact of how eagerly Pam is to join in Jim's prank and becomes a huge participant, like in pulling this prank on Dwight.

783
00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:52.820
Oh, yes.

784
00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:53.300
Yes.

785
00:28:53.300 --> 00:28:57.960
Well, this is their opportunity to spend time together, any opportunity to team up with Jim.

786
00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:03.800
I mean, in the deleted scene, we establish that the phone rings very infrequently.

787
00:29:04.140 --> 00:29:04.480
Yeah.

788
00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:06.840
Which we could probably have guessed anyway.

789
00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:08.660
This is so much fun for her.

790
00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:12.320
She is having so much fun, and Jim is just loving it.

791
00:29:12.420 --> 00:29:18.420
And his talking head about, like, how brilliant you were that you just came up, Pam came up with that all on her own.

792
00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:21.420
Yeah, when I come up to his desk and I'm, like, all fraught with it.

793
00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:22.580
Oh, I just don't know.

794
00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:27.600
Michael's told me so many things, and I want to unload all these secrets, but he told me not to.

795
00:29:27.960 --> 00:29:31.000
And I'm doing that all so that Dwight can overhear it.

796
00:29:31.180 --> 00:29:32.060
Dwight's, like, jackpot.

797
00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:35.440
But Jim is just so enamored with Pam in this scene.

798
00:29:35.540 --> 00:29:36.240
And you know what?

799
00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:37.680
I'm a jam fan, you guys.

800
00:29:37.680 --> 00:29:38.320
I am.

801
00:29:38.500 --> 00:29:39.900
And I love their storyline.

802
00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.500
I was just, like, a total fan of them.

803
00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:46.540
And I remember, like, every little nugget they had together.

804
00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:47.520
I was like, oh, my God!

805
00:29:47.940 --> 00:29:49.940
And this is definitely one of those episodes.

806
00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:51.200
Should we take a break?

807
00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:52.140
I don't know.

808
00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:53.120
Do you want to take a break?

809
00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:54.600
Please take us to a break.

810
00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:55.840
Oh, my God.

811
00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:02.000
We're back.

812
00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:02.360
Okay.

813
00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:02.900
All right.

814
00:30:03.020 --> 00:30:11.460
So another big part of this episode is Oscar baiting Michael into donating to his nephew's walk-a-thon charity.

815
00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:16.680
But Michael sort of not getting what a walk-a-thon is.

816
00:30:16.820 --> 00:30:16.960
No.

817
00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:19.360
So he puts in this big bid.

818
00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:26.260
And this is an example, also, of the benefit of the documentary crew element of the show.

819
00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:34.660
Because you can tell at first when Oscar walks into his office and asks him if he'll donate to his nephew's charity, he doesn't want to.

820
00:30:34.660 --> 00:30:38.240
Like, he would, if the cameras weren't there, he would probably say no.

821
00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:38.800
Right, right.

822
00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:39.980
But he can't say no.

823
00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:41.060
He wants to look like a good guy.

824
00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:42.500
He wants to look like a good guy.

825
00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:47.400
And so then he goes over the top and he donates $25.

826
00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:55.660
And only later is it brought to his attention that it's $25 per mile.

827
00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:56.900
Yes, exactly.

828
00:30:57.180 --> 00:31:02.220
And that realization happens in a great scene with Jim at the birthday party.

829
00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:05.060
And there is just this long pause.

830
00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:07.240
I'll tell you what.

831
00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:12.160
Steve Carell is better than anyone at letting an emotion wash across his face.

832
00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:15.900
And you watch that emotion of like, oh, crap.

833
00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:18.340
Like, just like, oh, what'd I do?

834
00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:19.800
Like, go across his face.

835
00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:22.720
And I feel like we see this again in Fun Run, right?

836
00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:27.320
Because he like signs up to do like so many miles and he doesn't realize.

837
00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:31.920
Something about Michael does not understand the concept of a walk-a-thon.

838
00:31:31.940 --> 00:31:33.660
A walk-a-thon or anything like that.

839
00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:34.920
But no, that is so good.

840
00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:35.900
Well, I did.

841
00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:45.060
I added it up because in the episode, Michael, once he realizes it's per mile, he asks Oscar like, hey, how strong is your nephew?

842
00:31:45.060 --> 00:31:46.160
Like, is he in good shape?

843
00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:47.500
He did 18 miles.

844
00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:48.000
He did 18 miles.

845
00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:50.220
So Michael has pledged $450.

846
00:31:51.080 --> 00:31:51.980
I did the math.

847
00:31:52.100 --> 00:31:53.920
He thinks he's pledged $25.

848
00:31:54.760 --> 00:31:57.100
But instead, he pledged $450.

849
00:31:57.560 --> 00:31:58.540
I also need to go back.

850
00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:01.260
I skipped something that I think is really a fun thing.

851
00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:04.820
In the parking lot, there's a scene where Jim and Dwight.

852
00:32:04.820 --> 00:32:06.040
Oh, I know what you're going to say.

853
00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:06.720
What am I going to say?

854
00:32:07.060 --> 00:32:08.080
It was Phyllis' car.

855
00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:09.420
It was Phyllis' car.

856
00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:09.960
Yes.

857
00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:16.220
Jim and Dwight are standing next to a car, and they are comparing notes about everybody in the office and what they've learned.

858
00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:19.000
And they're standing next to a car, and it's Phyllis' car.

859
00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:19.420
Yes.

860
00:32:19.560 --> 00:32:22.820
And Rain, as Dwight, keeps kicking the car.

861
00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:23.240
Yeah.

862
00:32:23.660 --> 00:32:24.860
And Phyllis—

863
00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:25.660
She didn't care for it.

864
00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:27.760
She was very nervous about it.

865
00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:34.340
And I guess at some point, she went up to Rain and said, listen, it's totally fine if you kick my car.

866
00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:36.520
But don't kick the car or the hubcap.

867
00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:37.440
Just the tire.

868
00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:38.180
Just the tire.

869
00:32:38.180 --> 00:32:41.520
Or we used to park our cars in that little parking lot.

870
00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:41.840
Yeah.

871
00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:42.640
And then—

872
00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:46.720
A lot of our real cars have appeared as cars in the show.

873
00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:49.940
And then they—in seasons later, they had us move our cars.

874
00:32:50.140 --> 00:32:52.960
I think maybe they realized there's, like, liability or something.

875
00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:57.100
I mean, so—but in those early days, those were our cars.

876
00:32:57.240 --> 00:33:07.820
A very, very big element of this episode is the point where Dwight ends up in a box in the warehouse.

877
00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:08.740
To spy.

878
00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:18.540
And more than any other question, when I put this out to fans for questions, the most questions that we got, the most frequently asked questions, were about this box.

879
00:33:18.760 --> 00:33:18.960
Right.

880
00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:20.900
How long was Rain in the box?

881
00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:22.660
How many takes did we do?

882
00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:24.940
How many boxes did we use?

883
00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:25.280
Right.

884
00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:33.060
One of the questions was there's a scene where Pam is on the phone doing a fake phone call, and the box kind of tips over.

885
00:33:33.260 --> 00:33:33.900
Wait, wait, wait, wait.

886
00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:35.000
And I laugh and run off.

887
00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:36.100
Wait, I have a note card.

888
00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:37.500
I have a note card about this.

889
00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:37.980
What is it?

890
00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:42.700
I want to—okay, I know you put it out to Twitter, but this is your BFF, so I get to—

891
00:33:42.700 --> 00:33:43.740
Can I ask you first?

892
00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:44.060
You get the first question.

893
00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:45.160
Jenna, question.

894
00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:45.860
Semi-colon.

895
00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:48.600
Did you really break and laugh by the box?

896
00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:52.280
I think you did, because I know what happens to your shoulders when you really laugh.

897
00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:53.560
This was the question.

898
00:33:53.780 --> 00:33:53.980
Ah!

899
00:33:53.980 --> 00:33:56.260
This is the question that people want to know.

900
00:33:57.160 --> 00:33:58.660
I think I faked it.

901
00:33:58.860 --> 00:33:59.300
What?

902
00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:01.080
Yeah, I don't think I really broke.

903
00:34:01.320 --> 00:34:02.600
I don't want to believe that.

904
00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:03.140
I know.

905
00:34:03.400 --> 00:34:09.080
I think—because I remember shooting that scene, and I remember it was very technical.

906
00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:14.200
So it was—I—it was happening over my shoulder.

907
00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:22.080
Stuff was happening over my shoulder, but there was a way where they wanted me to break at a certain moment, so it was kind of technical.

908
00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:24.880
So you had to sort of get out of the way, and the box was tipping.

909
00:34:25.120 --> 00:34:25.640
Yes, exactly.

910
00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:32.120
So I remember shooting it a number of times, and I remember thinking to myself, gosh, does this even look real?

911
00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:37.260
Like, do I—it's a really—there are two things that I think are really hard to do when you're acting.

912
00:34:37.420 --> 00:34:38.400
One of them is cry.

913
00:34:38.640 --> 00:34:38.960
Right.

914
00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:40.360
But I think people would expect that.

915
00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:42.080
But the other is to laugh convincingly.

916
00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:42.920
Yes.

917
00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.860
You know who has the best fake laugh is Steve Carell.

918
00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:46.380
Oh.

919
00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:50.780
Steve Carell can do this fake laugh that is the most honest, genuine laugh.

920
00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:56.380
And every time he would do it, I would start to laugh, even though I knew it was a fake laugh.

921
00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:57.740
It was so contagious.

922
00:34:58.360 --> 00:34:59.500
He—he is the best at it.

923
00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:04.460
I find it's easier to cry than to fake laugh myself for acting.

924
00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:05.560
Fake laughing is very difficult.

925
00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:06.320
It is.

926
00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:14.100
The reason I think that I didn't really break is because I remember having anxiety over whether or not anyone was going to buy it.

927
00:35:14.480 --> 00:35:16.860
Jenna, I have a little something on my note card here.

928
00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:17.500
What is it?

929
00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:21.840
I have another note card I wrote, this is the first time we see Daryl.

930
00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:22.420
Yes.

931
00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:23.360
Yes.

932
00:35:23.540 --> 00:35:28.080
When rain pops out of the box at the end, Daryl is down there.

933
00:35:28.280 --> 00:35:28.680
Yes.

934
00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:30.640
Now, he is not going to have a big scene.

935
00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:33.920
We're not going to hear him speak until the next episode, which is basketball.

936
00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:34.580
Yes.

937
00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:37.300
But that is the first time we see Daryl, so I was very excited.

938
00:35:37.500 --> 00:35:37.840
I know.

939
00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:39.740
And look, my note card came in handy.

940
00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:41.220
I love that about her.

941
00:35:41.260 --> 00:35:44.580
I love how we plant seeds that grow later.

942
00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:45.060
Right.

943
00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:54.800
We don't – that is a person who is going to be a major character on the show, but we introduce him just as a guy seeing rain come out of the box.

944
00:35:55.020 --> 00:35:55.200
Yeah.

945
00:35:55.400 --> 00:36:01.180
That is one of the things, and I know we say it over and over, and we will for the life of this podcast,

946
00:36:01.180 --> 00:36:06.140
but one of the things I love about what Greg Daniels did is he starts in the middle.

947
00:36:06.140 --> 00:36:10.580
He doesn't, like, spoon-feed you information, just the way they design the show.

948
00:36:10.760 --> 00:36:12.460
You play catch-up as an audience member.

949
00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:16.140
You're just, like, a day in the life, and you're right in the middle, and I love that.

950
00:36:16.280 --> 00:36:16.480
Yes.

951
00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:17.340
I love that, too.

952
00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:17.980
Okay.

953
00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:20.480
Here's a big confession about the box.

954
00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:22.020
This is going to blow your mind.

955
00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:22.460
What?

956
00:36:24.100 --> 00:36:27.040
Rain wasn't in the box most of the time.

957
00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:28.520
Who was in the box?

958
00:36:28.660 --> 00:36:29.800
Who was in the box?

959
00:36:29.800 --> 00:36:30.460
Who was in the box?

960
00:36:30.460 --> 00:36:32.680
Phil Shea, our props.

961
00:36:32.700 --> 00:36:33.340
Prop guy.

962
00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:33.660
Yeah.

963
00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:34.600
Phil Shea.

964
00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:34.900
Yeah.

965
00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:36.500
Phil Shea is a very tall person.

966
00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:37.600
Yeah, well, so is Rain.

967
00:36:37.860 --> 00:36:39.740
And he was in that box all the time.

968
00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:45.660
So most of the box acting was done by Phil Shea, of course, except for the end where Rain busts out.

969
00:36:45.740 --> 00:36:46.080
And comes out.

970
00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:50.660
So in that scene where I'm cracking up, Phil Shea was in the box.

971
00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:51.380
Oh, my gosh.

972
00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:52.660
You know what I think we should do, Angela?

973
00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:53.540
Tell me, Jenna.

974
00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:59.120
I think we should call Phil Shea and get him to answer all of our questions about the box.

975
00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:00.360
What was it like in the box?

976
00:37:00.460 --> 00:37:02.640
Phil Shea, we are about to blow up your phone.

977
00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:06.280
Phil?

978
00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:06.960
Yes.

979
00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:08.740
Phil Shea, it's Jenna and Angela.

980
00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:09.660
Hi, Phil.

981
00:37:10.580 --> 00:37:11.380
Oh, hey, guys.

982
00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:11.860
How are you?

983
00:37:12.040 --> 00:37:12.300
Good.

984
00:37:12.380 --> 00:37:12.960
How are you?

985
00:37:13.360 --> 00:37:15.920
Thank you so much for letting us call and bug you.

986
00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:19.520
It's always a pleasure to help out Pam and Angela.

987
00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:20.960
Oh, my gosh.

988
00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:22.440
Oh, Phil, thank you so much.

989
00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:24.340
It's just so nice to hear your voice, I have to say.

990
00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:25.360
That's what I was thinking.

991
00:37:25.620 --> 00:37:27.440
I was like, gosh, it's so nice to hear your voice.

992
00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:30.700
Hey, what an experience we had for nine years, right?

993
00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:32.920
Yes, we were the luckieSt.

994
00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:33.960
We were the luckieSt.

995
00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:38.820
Well, if you have a second, Phil, can we ask you some questions from the Alliance episode?

996
00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:40.360
Absolutely.

997
00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:50.240
So we opened up questions to the fans, and overwhelmingly, the thing people wanted to know about was the box that Rain gets into in the warehouse.

998
00:37:51.300 --> 00:37:52.380
Oh, my gosh.

999
00:37:52.380 --> 00:37:56.620
They wanted to know how long he was in the box, everything about the box.

1000
00:37:56.780 --> 00:37:59.220
But, Phil, I know you were in the box.

1001
00:37:59.700 --> 00:38:05.860
Rain only got in after all the shooing and the scooching and the sliding and then the tumbling was done.

1002
00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:08.300
Phil, you did all of that in the box?

1003
00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:10.820
You were the scoocher and the tumbler and all that?

1004
00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:12.260
I was.

1005
00:38:12.640 --> 00:38:20.480
Apparently, Mr. Zabornak, our line producer, didn't think we had enough money to hire a stunt guy, so he assigned me to be in the box.

1006
00:38:20.480 --> 00:38:24.040
And I remember I had to go out and buy my own elbow pads and knee pads.

1007
00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:35.140
And then I had a loose walkie-talkie in there with me with Liz Ryan, who was the first AP, shouting out all of Brian Gordon, who was our director for that episode's command.

1008
00:38:35.560 --> 00:38:37.200
I remember like it was yesterday.

1009
00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:39.780
So, Phil, can you take me through it from the beginning?

1010
00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:44.060
You get a script, and it says this character has to be in a box.

1011
00:38:44.420 --> 00:38:45.560
So what do you do?

1012
00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:48.920
Because you're in charge of getting the box as the prop master.

1013
00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:50.360
So where do you start?

1014
00:38:50.360 --> 00:39:01.960
On that particular prop, I sought out Greg Daniels, and I said, do you see Rain sitting down squatting in the box, or is he kneeling down on his hands and knees?

1015
00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:03.620
And he said, kneeling on his hands and knees.

1016
00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:05.120
And Rain is tall.

1017
00:39:05.280 --> 00:39:06.240
Rain is tall, right?

1018
00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:07.580
And so are you, Phil.

1019
00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:08.440
You're very tall.

1020
00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:08.860
Yeah.

1021
00:39:09.420 --> 00:39:11.420
Yeah, Rain is, I think, 6'1 or 6'2.

1022
00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:13.440
I'm 6'1 if I blow dry my hair.

1023
00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:15.780
You do have great hair.

1024
00:39:16.520 --> 00:39:18.860
You guys should know, Phil has an amazing head of hair.

1025
00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:19.480
He really does.

1026
00:39:19.620 --> 00:39:20.260
This is true.

1027
00:39:20.360 --> 00:39:21.120
I leased it.

1028
00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:21.740
I leased it.

1029
00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:22.920
It's a Hollywood fake hairdo.

1030
00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:29.240
But I tracked Rain down, and I asked him to get on his hands and knees, and I was measuring for this box.

1031
00:39:29.480 --> 00:39:32.960
Wait, you made Rain get on his hands and knees, and then you measured him?

1032
00:39:33.140 --> 00:39:34.720
And you measured him to know how big it was?

1033
00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:36.140
That was season one.

1034
00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:37.420
Rain was willing to do anything.

1035
00:39:37.620 --> 00:39:42.680
Then I went to a couple box cities around Southern California, and no one had a box close to that.

1036
00:39:43.240 --> 00:39:48.120
Then I went on to the internet, and I looked at box manufacturers in Southern California.

1037
00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:54.000
I found a company in San Diego named Kent Landsberg.

1038
00:39:54.280 --> 00:40:00.260
And you have to understand, as prop people, we have a very short production time and lead time to get all these props.

1039
00:40:00.260 --> 00:40:04.380
So I called this Kent Landsberg paper company and asked to speak to the manager.

1040
00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:08.920
And in a much more frenetic and hyper voice, I said, look, you've got to help me out.

1041
00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:10.580
I have to have a custom box made.

1042
00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:14.140
I probably need about 100 of them, and they need to be this dimension.

1043
00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:17.580
And San Diego is like three hours away, too.

1044
00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:19.760
Well, and also, that's such a good point.

1045
00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:21.480
You didn't need one box.

1046
00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:22.580
You needed 100.

1047
00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:25.760
You needed 100 boxes, because you didn't know how many takes we were going to do.

1048
00:40:25.900 --> 00:40:31.560
And Brian Gordon, the director of this episode, ended up being notorious for doing tons of takes.

1049
00:40:31.820 --> 00:40:35.080
So you were right to cover your bases and get 100.

1050
00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:35.840
Yes.

1051
00:40:36.340 --> 00:40:36.740
Right.

1052
00:40:36.880 --> 00:40:39.320
And we shot that scene in the Dunder Mifflin warehouse.

1053
00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:41.320
We wanted to have some boxes on the shelves.

1054
00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:42.800
Oh, right.

1055
00:40:42.800 --> 00:40:44.620
Right, just ones that he didn't bust out of.

1056
00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:45.480
Yeah.

1057
00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:45.780
Right.

1058
00:40:45.800 --> 00:40:46.280
Of course.

1059
00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:47.900
It wasn't a prop box.

1060
00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:48.940
It just appeared out of nowhere.

1061
00:40:48.940 --> 00:40:52.180
So we had to give some legitimacy to where it originated from.

1062
00:40:52.260 --> 00:40:53.360
Look at all the detail.

1063
00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:54.580
Look at all the details.

1064
00:40:54.580 --> 00:40:55.860
So how did you do it?

1065
00:40:55.980 --> 00:40:56.620
So how did you do it?

1066
00:40:56.660 --> 00:40:58.280
This guy said he would do it, no problem.

1067
00:40:58.460 --> 00:40:59.540
And then how did you get them?

1068
00:41:00.680 --> 00:41:01.760
Well, I talked to him.

1069
00:41:01.820 --> 00:41:03.740
He said the lead time is usually a month and a half.

1070
00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:05.300
I said, we have four days.

1071
00:41:05.320 --> 00:41:05.660
Oh, no.

1072
00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:07.500
I said, I need the boxes in four days.

1073
00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:08.840
And I said, it was for a TV show.

1074
00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:11.800
He said, it's for The Office, which is a remake of the British version.

1075
00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:15.240
And he probably said, I'm sorry, I'm a huge fan of the British version.

1076
00:41:15.340 --> 00:41:16.580
And I'm not interested in helping you.

1077
00:41:16.580 --> 00:41:17.140
I've never heard of your show.

1078
00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:17.460
Yeah.

1079
00:41:18.820 --> 00:41:20.620
It's like you guys were listening into the phone.

1080
00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:21.800
That's exactly what he said.

1081
00:41:21.880 --> 00:41:22.320
No, no.

1082
00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:24.900
He said he would be glad to make an exception for a TV show.

1083
00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:25.900
So he made it happen.

1084
00:41:26.020 --> 00:41:30.340
We got the boxes delivered to Culver City, which is where we shot that episode in our first season.

1085
00:41:30.520 --> 00:41:32.980
And then they put the graphics on the box.

1086
00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:35.180
And we had them back two days before we were filming.

1087
00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:37.940
So we had it all done and ranged it in the box fine.

1088
00:41:37.940 --> 00:41:45.220
And then it was just about coordinating where the holes would be inserted by me when I'm inside the box with Dwight's Swiss Army knife.

1089
00:41:45.360 --> 00:41:47.240
And it was a great scene.

1090
00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:49.120
I mean, I remember we shot that down.

1091
00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:50.980
I think it was a Friday night we shot that scene.

1092
00:41:51.080 --> 00:41:51.840
But it was fun.

1093
00:41:51.940 --> 00:41:52.780
Everybody was laughing.

1094
00:41:53.020 --> 00:41:54.980
The crew was always laughing on that show.

1095
00:41:54.980 --> 00:41:57.000
And it was a great scene.

1096
00:41:57.180 --> 00:41:58.800
And fans love it so much.

1097
00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:00.640
Oh, Phil, thank you so much.

1098
00:42:01.080 --> 00:42:06.120
I'm looking at a picture that I can email you that Michael Gallenberg gave me.

1099
00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:11.040
I'm going to send you a picture as soon as we hang up of me getting ready to tape up the Swiss box.

1100
00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:15.700
And I don't know if you have the ability to post the picture on the podcaSt.

1101
00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:17.140
But I'm going to take a picture.

1102
00:42:17.140 --> 00:42:18.560
We can put it on our website, maybe.

1103
00:42:19.080 --> 00:42:21.260
Okay, I'll take a picture.

1104
00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:24.700
And you can see by the expression on my face after about take 50.

1105
00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:28.060
You were like in a sweat lodge.

1106
00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:30.300
Oh, Phil, thank you.

1107
00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:32.380
But Jenna, you were there.

1108
00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:35.700
And you remember the last take when Brian Gordon said one more.

1109
00:42:35.780 --> 00:42:36.580
I hope you remember this.

1110
00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:38.680
Where he had me do all this stuff.

1111
00:42:38.800 --> 00:42:42.100
And then when I cut myself out of the box, everybody had went and hit.

1112
00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:43.880
Oh, that's right.

1113
00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:49.160
I came out into the warehouse, so he at least played a practical joke on me at the end to make me laugh.

1114
00:42:49.240 --> 00:42:49.980
So that was pretty funny.

1115
00:42:50.120 --> 00:42:50.520
We did.

1116
00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:53.880
We left you in the box, and we pretended like we were still filming.

1117
00:42:54.200 --> 00:42:56.980
And then you just were there for so long.

1118
00:42:57.040 --> 00:42:57.980
That is horrible.

1119
00:42:58.120 --> 00:42:59.460
That is kind of horrible.

1120
00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:00.720
Why were we so mean?

1121
00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:04.300
Phil, if I was there, Phil, I would have come up and said, Phil, get out.

1122
00:43:04.380 --> 00:43:04.840
They left.

1123
00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:06.680
It was so funny.

1124
00:43:06.780 --> 00:43:07.300
No, it was funny.

1125
00:43:07.300 --> 00:43:07.700
It was very funny.

1126
00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:08.440
It was a great scene.

1127
00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:13.320
Phil, can I throw two questions at you really quickly before we let you go?

1128
00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:13.880
Oh, okay.

1129
00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:14.160
Okay.

1130
00:43:14.620 --> 00:43:14.800
Yeah.

1131
00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:20.240
So after getting this box sounds like it was a massive undertaking.

1132
00:43:20.540 --> 00:43:22.600
Phil, your job is sounding really fun.

1133
00:43:22.820 --> 00:43:28.960
Like you read an episode, and then you have to come up with all these weird objects and gags.

1134
00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:31.140
It sounds fun and stressful, Phil.

1135
00:43:31.480 --> 00:43:34.680
Like how do you get 100 boxes in four days?

1136
00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:37.000
It's always fun.

1137
00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:38.160
It's always stressful.

1138
00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:47.000
And it's great to be, like I said, a small part of a production that resonates with viewers and fans across generations as The Office did.

1139
00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:49.160
So it is fun.

1140
00:43:49.260 --> 00:43:49.880
It is stressful.

1141
00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:57.920
But it's a real joy to do it when everybody laughs and everybody enjoys the prop, and it adds a little value to the scripted scene.

1142
00:43:58.080 --> 00:44:00.840
And then we all go home and laugh and had a good day.

1143
00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:01.540
So, yeah.

1144
00:44:01.860 --> 00:44:06.280
Well, we sure appreciate you, and we are going to be calling you a lot, The Office Lady.

1145
00:44:06.460 --> 00:44:07.380
So thank you.

1146
00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:08.820
Thanks for taking the time, Phil.

1147
00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:12.680
I have vivid memories of all the props we made for The Office.

1148
00:44:12.820 --> 00:44:15.300
So anytime you guys want to call me, get some behind the scenes.

1149
00:44:15.560 --> 00:44:15.610
Okay.

1150
00:44:15.860 --> 00:44:16.260
Anytime.

1151
00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:16.740
Seriously.

1152
00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:20.320
As you were talking, I had like eight questions about different episodes down the road.

1153
00:44:20.580 --> 00:44:21.360
I did, too.

1154
00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:23.540
So we will definitely be calling you back, Phil.

1155
00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:24.800
Thank you so much.

1156
00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:26.100
We miss you.

1157
00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:27.200
Let's keep Dunder Mifflin going.

1158
00:44:27.460 --> 00:44:28.500
I miss you as well.

1159
00:44:28.740 --> 00:44:31.420
Let's keep Dunder Mifflin relevant for the next 30 years.

1160
00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:31.980
Yay.

1161
00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:33.040
All right, Phil.

1162
00:44:33.080 --> 00:44:34.760
Well, have a good day.

1163
00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:35.260
Yeah.

1164
00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:36.840
Tell everyone we say hi over there.

1165
00:44:37.040 --> 00:44:37.220
Okay.

1166
00:44:37.840 --> 00:44:38.320
I will.

1167
00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:38.840
Thanks, Angela.

1168
00:44:39.000 --> 00:44:39.260
Thanks, Angela.

1169
00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:39.640
All right.

1170
00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:40.040
Bye.

1171
00:44:40.200 --> 00:44:40.540
Bye.

1172
00:44:40.760 --> 00:44:41.080
Thank you.

1173
00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:41.380
Bye.

1174
00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:42.900
What's next, Jenna?

1175
00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:44.520
I have three note cards left.

1176
00:44:44.720 --> 00:44:46.720
Well, now we need to talk about the cake.

1177
00:44:47.020 --> 00:44:47.960
Oh, yes.

1178
00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:51.400
The ice cream, the mint chocolate chip ice cream cake.

1179
00:44:51.620 --> 00:44:52.020
Yes.

1180
00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:54.480
I got so sick reading that cake.

1181
00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:59.640
This is the second most frequently asked question was about the cake and what the cake was like

1182
00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:00.960
and did we really eat the cake?

1183
00:45:01.160 --> 00:45:02.880
Oh, we ate that cake, you guys.

1184
00:45:03.060 --> 00:45:03.540
And guess what?

1185
00:45:03.660 --> 00:45:04.440
You start filming.

1186
00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:07.480
We would start filming at 7.30 a.m.

1187
00:45:07.600 --> 00:45:07.740
Yeah.

1188
00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:11.080
So at 7.30 a.m., you're going to eat ice cream cake?

1189
00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:13.940
Someone hands you a plate of ice cream cake at 7.30 a.m.

1190
00:45:13.940 --> 00:45:15.840
And you're going to eat it for five hours?

1191
00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:16.460
All day.

1192
00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:16.840
All day.

1193
00:45:16.840 --> 00:45:18.900
And then they're like, let's break for lunch.

1194
00:45:19.040 --> 00:45:20.500
And you're like, I want to puke.

1195
00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:20.900
Yeah.

1196
00:45:21.120 --> 00:45:22.940
Let me say something about prop food.

1197
00:45:23.420 --> 00:45:24.220
Oh, do it.

1198
00:45:24.220 --> 00:45:25.980
But BJ Novak loves prop food.

1199
00:45:26.180 --> 00:45:27.840
Mindy Kaling loves prop food.

1200
00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:29.320
I also kind of like it.

1201
00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:31.620
I get real excited when I have to snack in a scene.

1202
00:45:31.780 --> 00:45:34.080
I do not because I know it's going to turn on me.

1203
00:45:34.180 --> 00:45:34.540
I know.

1204
00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:36.540
And it turns on me every time.

1205
00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:37.020
Every time.

1206
00:45:37.220 --> 00:45:38.620
And I still make the same mistakes.

1207
00:45:38.820 --> 00:45:39.220
Every time.

1208
00:45:39.300 --> 00:45:43.600
I would see Ed Helms, Mindy Kaling, you, BJ be like, ooh, prop food and get so excited.

1209
00:45:43.660 --> 00:45:43.780
Yeah.

1210
00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:45.280
Like, guys, pace yourself.

1211
00:45:45.460 --> 00:45:46.280
It's going to get gross.

1212
00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:49.280
We'd have a party in the conference room.

1213
00:45:49.440 --> 00:45:50.960
And they'd have, like, pigs in a blanket.

1214
00:45:51.320 --> 00:45:53.820
And you just, me and BJ would be over there.

1215
00:45:53.980 --> 00:45:55.620
I'd be like, guys, slow down.

1216
00:45:55.700 --> 00:45:56.100
I know.

1217
00:45:56.200 --> 00:45:58.180
You're going to have to eat that for four hours.

1218
00:45:58.440 --> 00:45:58.740
I know.

1219
00:45:58.820 --> 00:46:01.620
Well, this was the first time that I made the mistake.

1220
00:46:01.860 --> 00:46:05.120
And I started wolfing down ice cream cake early.

1221
00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:07.300
And then you have to match that in every shot.

1222
00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:10.860
So whatever you do in that first shot, you then have to match it.

1223
00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:13.500
So continuity-wise, it all makes sense.

1224
00:46:13.640 --> 00:46:14.300
And here's the thing.

1225
00:46:14.380 --> 00:46:18.100
I'm in the background of most of the ice cream eating scenes.

1226
00:46:18.260 --> 00:46:18.820
What are you thinking?

1227
00:46:18.820 --> 00:46:21.520
So I was wolfing it down in the background.

1228
00:46:21.520 --> 00:46:22.600
She didn't have to.

1229
00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:23.680
She was in the background.

1230
00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:24.560
I was in the background.

1231
00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:29.580
And then by the time it was time for my close-up, I was so sick.

1232
00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:32.160
I didn't want to eat the ice cream cake.

1233
00:46:32.160 --> 00:46:33.360
But then I had to.

1234
00:46:33.860 --> 00:46:34.760
I'll tell you this.

1235
00:46:34.800 --> 00:46:35.800
I had a slice.

1236
00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:37.780
And Michael walks up to Phyllis and I.

1237
00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:43.020
And when he walks up, poor Steve is like wolfing the cake down through as he's talking.

1238
00:46:43.240 --> 00:46:45.880
He would eat a full piece every take.

1239
00:46:46.020 --> 00:46:46.360
Oh, man.

1240
00:46:46.500 --> 00:46:50.940
They eventually got a bucket, though, that he could spit in, which is also disgusting.

1241
00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:51.860
It's called a spit bucket.

1242
00:46:51.860 --> 00:46:52.780
It's called a spit bucket.

1243
00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:55.200
But he would walk up eating the cake.

1244
00:46:55.200 --> 00:46:57.980
And he was talking to Phyllis and Meredith and my character.

1245
00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:01.380
And I was just going to take one bite as he walked up.

1246
00:47:01.760 --> 00:47:07.080
And by the end of the five hours of doing the scene, when I would scoop up that one bite,

1247
00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:10.660
my hand would start to shake because I was anticipating it.

1248
00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:11.480
So I was like, oh.

1249
00:47:12.640 --> 00:47:14.620
Yes, your body was rejecting it.

1250
00:47:14.620 --> 00:47:16.420
My body was rejecting it.

1251
00:47:16.540 --> 00:47:20.860
It was like, please don't put that mint chocolate chip ice cream in your mouth one more time ever

1252
00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:21.920
again for the rest of your life.

1253
00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:22.560
I can't eat it.

1254
00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:23.400
I can't eat it.

1255
00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:24.760
I can't eat it now.

1256
00:47:25.080 --> 00:47:26.620
My daughter loves mint chocolate chip.

1257
00:47:26.740 --> 00:47:27.780
I don't want it near me.

1258
00:47:27.880 --> 00:47:30.480
I loved ice cream cake in general.

1259
00:47:30.480 --> 00:47:30.920
No.

1260
00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:37.960
And only, it was last year for my son's seventh birthday, I finally ate a piece of ice cream

1261
00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:38.640
cake again.

1262
00:47:38.920 --> 00:47:42.960
But that's, it had been like I'd gone on like a 15-year moratorium.

1263
00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:45.040
So the final thing I think we should talk about.

1264
00:47:45.060 --> 00:47:45.340
Oh, yeah.

1265
00:47:45.440 --> 00:47:45.560
Let's go.

1266
00:47:45.560 --> 00:47:46.140
With this episode.

1267
00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:46.760
On that note.

1268
00:47:47.220 --> 00:47:52.300
Is the scene with Roy coming into the office and confronting Jim.

1269
00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:54.040
You guys are so busted.

1270
00:47:54.500 --> 00:47:57.000
I remember like being over in accounting being like, oh.

1271
00:47:57.160 --> 00:48:00.640
And also, David Dinman can be scary when he wants to be.

1272
00:48:00.640 --> 00:48:01.300
He can be intimidating.

1273
00:48:01.700 --> 00:48:03.080
He's such a teddy bear in real life.

1274
00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:03.540
I know.

1275
00:48:03.640 --> 00:48:04.580
